{\rtf1\ansi\deff0{\fonttbl{\f0 Arial;}}\fs22\b Requirements Table - Lab 1\b0\par Requirements Table - Lab 1\par Problem Statement #47: Domain & SSL Certificate Expiry Alert System\par Developer Tools & IT Operations\par Req ID | Type | Description (The system shall...) | Priority | Acceptance Criteria | Rationale\par FR-001 | Functional | The system shall initiate TLS handshakes against monitored domains daily, extract certificate expiration dates, and trigger alerts at 30, 15, and 3 days before expiry. | High | Pass: Alert is generated at 30, 15, and 3 days before a certificate expiry date. Fail: No alert is generated or the wrong threshold is used. | Directly implements the core certificate-expiry monitoring requirement and reduces service disruption risk.\par FR-002 | Functional | The system shall perform daily WHOIS lookups for monitored domains and identify domain registration expiration dates. | High | Pass: WHOIS data and a registration expiry date are stored for every monitored domain. Fail: Any monitored domain has missing or stale WHOIS expiry data after a successful lookup. | Domain expiration is a separate availability and security risk that must be monitored alongside certificates.\par FR-003 | Functional | The system shall allow a SysAdmin to add, edit, delete, and view the list of monitored domains and SSL endpoints. | Medium | Pass: A change to the monitored list is reflected in the next audit and is visible on the dashboard. Fail: A saved change is missing or an unintended endpoint remains monitored. | Accurate monitoring scope is required to ensure complete and current coverage.\par FR-004 | Functional | The system shall escalate expiry alerts through the configured escalation ladder until the alert is acknowledged or the final escalation level is reached. | High | Pass: Alerts progress through each configured level when not acknowledged. Fail: An escalation level is skipped or escalation stops before acknowledgement without reaching the final level. | Ensures time-critical expiry warnings reach responsible personnel even when the first recipient does not respond.\par FR-005 | Functional | The system shall provide a dashboard showing current domain and certificate expiry status, upcoming expirations, audit results, and alert history. | Medium | Pass: Dashboard loads with current status and supports filtering/searching of monitored endpoints. Fail: Status is stale, missing, or filtering returns incorrect results. | Provides SysAdmins and Security Officers with visibility for proactive maintenance and review.\par NFR-001 | Nonfunctional - Performance & Scalability | The monitoring engine shall scan a list of 1,000 domains and SSL endpoints in under 3 minutes. | High | Pass: Benchmarking confirms completion for 1,000 endpoints in less than 3 minutes under the defined test load. Fail: The run takes 3 minutes or longer. | Supports timely daily audits as the monitored environment grows.\par NFR-002 | Nonfunctional - Security | The system shall store credentials and other sensitive monitoring configuration securely using encryption and least-privilege access controls. | High | Pass: Secrets are encrypted at rest and in transit, and access-audit tests show only authorized roles can retrieve or modify them. Fail: Secrets are stored in plaintext or unauthorized access is permitted. | Protects operational credentials and sensitive monitoring data from unauthorized exposure.}